Agreed and done in 9e12daf. The field genuinely carries two meanings now and the interface only described one, which is the wrong place for that gap to sit — a second consumer reads the type, not the branch.

```ts
// True when this folder's subfolders were truncated, OR when the item budget
// ran out before the folder was read at all. Both render as a trailing `...`,
// which is the point: an unread folder must not be indistinguishable from one
// that was read and found empty. Any new consumer has to cover the second
// case too — gating on `subFolders.length > 0` drops it silently.
hasMoreSubfolders?: boolean;
```

I kept your `subFolders.length > 0` example in the comment, since naming the specific wrong gate is more useful to the next reader than restating the invariant.

`getFolderStructure.test.ts` 17/17, prettier and eslint clean. Comment-only, so your local verification of the behaviour still stands unchanged.

Separately — thank you for the build-and-run verification report. The 18 real directories in `packages/core/src` rendering as bare leaves at the production default is a much better argument for this change than anything in my description, and the `packages/core/src/tools` control showing byte-for-byte identical output is exactly the over-correction check I should have included myself.

